<h2>Why the Family Courts Act has its own evidence scheme</h2>
<p>The Family Courts Act, 1984 was enacted to create specialised courts for disputes relating to marriage and family affairs, with a statutory mandate to attempt settlement first and to adopt a procedure less adversarial than the ordinary courts. That design would fail if family judges were bound hand and foot by technical rules of pleading, proof and admissibility. The Act therefore builds in a cluster of provisions that relax procedure and evidence, while leaving the substantive law — of divorce, maintenance, custody and property between spouses — untouched.</p>

<h2>Procedure and privacy: Sections 10 and 11</h2>
<p><strong>Section 10</strong> applies the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 to suits and proceedings before a family court, and the code of criminal procedure to proceedings of a criminal character such as maintenance, but then adds the provision that matters most in practice: nothing prevents a family court from laying down its own procedure with a view to arriving at a settlement or at the truth of the facts alleged. The family court is thus master of its own procedure in a way an ordinary civil court is not.</p>
<p><strong>Section 11</strong> protects privacy. Proceedings may be held in camera if the court so desires, and <em>shall</em> be held in camera if either party so desires. In Delhi, where matrimonial evidence frequently involves intimate allegations, parties routinely invoke this provision during their own depositions.</p>

<h2>Representation: Section 13</h2>
<p><strong>Section 13</strong> states that no party to a suit or proceeding before a family court shall be entitled, as of right, to be represented by a legal practitioner, while enabling the court to secure the services of a legal expert as amicus curiae where it considers that necessary in the interest of justice. The provision reflects the Act’s conciliatory philosophy; in day-to-day practice, family courts in Delhi permit counsel to appear, and the section operates chiefly as a reminder that the proceeding belongs to the parties, not to the technicalities of adversarial trial.</p>

<h2>Section 14: the heart of the relaxation</h2>
<p><strong>Section 14</strong> is the provision that most changes the texture of a family court trial. It provides that a family court may receive as evidence any report, statement, documents, information or matter that may, in its opinion, assist it to deal effectually with a dispute — whether or not the same would be otherwise relevant or admissible under the Indian Evidence Act, 1872. Three features deserve emphasis:</p>
<div class="check"><ul>
<li>The gateway is <strong>assistance</strong>, not admissibility: the question is whether the material helps the court deal effectually with the dispute.</li>
<li>The power is <strong>discretionary</strong> — the court <em>may</em> receive the material; a party cannot force every scrap of paper onto the record.</li>
<li>The relaxation displaces the exclusionary rules of the general law of evidence, but it does not certify the material as true.</li>
</ul></div>
<p>Alongside Section 14 sit two companions on recording. <strong>Section 15</strong> dispenses with recording oral evidence at length: it is not necessary to record the evidence of witnesses in full, and the judge instead prepares a memorandum of the substance of what the witness deposes, signed by the witness and the judge, which forms part of the record. <strong>Section 16</strong> permits evidence of a formal character to be given by affidavit, subject to the court’s power to summon and examine the deponent as to the facts stated. For substantive examination-in-chief, family courts in Delhi follow the affidavit route familiar from the Code of Civil Procedure, with the witness then tendered for cross-examination.</p>

<h2>How the scheme plays out in Delhi: chats, recordings, photographs, statements</h2>
<p>In the family courts at Delhi’s district court complexes, Section 14 is the working answer to most objections against everyday digital material. WhatsApp conversations, call recordings, photographs, emails and bank statements are routinely taken on record in maintenance, custody and divorce proceedings because they plainly <em>assist</em> the court — in gauging income and lifestyle, the conduct of the parties, or the welfare of a child. A party is still well advised to file such material with a supporting affidavit identifying its source and, for electronic records, the certificate contemplated by the general law of evidence, because doing so strengthens the material even where Section 14 would excuse its absence.</p>

<h2>The limit: admissibility is relaxed, weight is not</h2>
<p>The most common misunderstanding about Section 14 is to read it as a guarantee that whatever is filed will be believed. It is not. The relaxation goes to <strong>admissibility</strong> — the threshold question of whether material may come on record at all. <strong>Weight</strong> — how much the material proves — remains fully arguable. A chat transcript of unknown provenance, a selectively edited recording, or a photograph without context may be received under Section 14 and yet carry little or no persuasive value once tested in cross-examination. Judgments of family courts are appealable to the High Court under Section 19 of the Act, and findings resting on flimsy material do not survive scrutiny merely because Section 14 allowed the material in.</p>

<h2>From the Indian Evidence Act to the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam</h2>
<p>Section 14 of the Family Courts Act refers in terms to the Indian Evidence Act, 1872. That Act stands repealed and replaced by the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 with effect from 1 July 2024, and under the ordinary rules of statutory construction the reference is now read as a reference to the corresponding new law. The BSA carries forward the familiar architecture — relevancy, admissions, documentary proof — and gives updated statutory treatment to electronic and digital records, including the certificate requirement for electronic evidence now housed in Section 63 BSA (formerly Section 65B of the Evidence Act). For family court practice the position is unchanged in substance: the general law supplies the default rules, and Section 14 of the Family Courts Act supplies the relaxation.</p>

<div class="note"><p>The practical lesson for litigants is twofold. First, do not withhold useful material out of fear that it is technically inadmissible — Section 14 usually gets it on record. Second, do not mistake reception for proof: material should still be filed with proper identification, source and certification wherever possible, because the contest in a family court is almost never about whether a document comes in, and almost always about what it is worth.</p></div>
